From dc4873e74ece40e9aacab493b0d39f08686f7fa7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 02:47:12 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] fix(sim): the field marches walk cell centres with exact
 signs, never points

The review of PR #817 found march_up's `phi == 0 => Floor(z)` exit (a copy
of march's) reading a flipped floor 0.25 high: a probe's magnitude is the
cell's, so a cell whose centre lies on a stock plane reads zero at every
point of the cell, and the exit answered whatever height the ascent had
reached. The same exit stands upright in march (#824). Two more holes
share the root in both marches: the sphere trace stepped by the cell's
magnitude from points between centres (a band value can over-read the
truth by up to sqrt(3) cells), so it could land past a web thinner than
a cell; and its "last air probe" fallback was no proof under a cut claim.

One shared `march::walk` now visits cell centres only, with the exact
parity-and-claim sign at each (the half-open [floor, top) law: a centre
on a floor plane is material, on a top plane air), skips only runs the
field proves air (a centre outside the shell: its magnitude's cells less
two; a claimed centre: its claim's cells), and reads the surface at the
first material centre from the one-cell bracket to the proven-air centre
before it (`face`, unchanged). `march` and `march_up` are the two
directions of that walk; `refine`, `refine_down`, `probe`, `STEP_MIN`
and `MAX_MARCH_STEPS` are gone. The sliver scan's upper bound follows
the same law (a centre on the ceiling is the one above it), so the
reviewer's scenario reads [5.8, 6.25] exactly through the record.

Pinned by field_floor::a_stock_plane_on_a_cell_centre_reads_exactly_on_both_sides
(the reviewer's flipped scenario, #824's upright twin, the uncut plate
both ways, a top on a centre) and
field_floor::no_column_under_reports_across_plates_and_slots_on_and_off_the_lattice
(4 floors x 3 tops x 9 slot depths, under and beside the slot, upright
and flipped: 432 reads, all exact, none under-read). Both fail on the
old marches. Every existing suite reads the same floats; the app's
release heavy lane agrees with the column engine on every golden.

Refs #780
Refs #824

